Give a daemonized valkey-server a GIL its own thread owns.

moduleInitModulesSystem() locks the modules' GIL early in main(), and the
event loop unlocks it before every sleep and locks it again after. With
--daemonize yes the server then fork()s, and the child goes on running the
event loop. On QNX the child's thread does not own the mutex its parent
locked: pthread_mutex_unlock() returns EPERM (hazard 127), and the next
pthread_mutex_lock() in afterSleep() blocks for good -- a daemonized
valkey-server accepted connections and never answered (seen with the static
Lua module loaded, i.e. always). Re-initialise the GIL in the daemonized
child and lock it again, which is the state the parent had.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/src/module.c
+++ b/src/module.c
@@ -9337,6 +9337,17 @@
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&moduleGIL);
 }
 
+/* After fork() the child's main thread is not the owner of the GIL its
+ * parent held: on QNX, unlocking it then fails with EPERM and the next lock
+ * blocks for good. A child that goes on running the event loop (daemonize)
+ * takes a fresh, locked GIL. */
+void moduleResetGILAfterFork(void) {
+#ifdef __QNX__
+    pthread_mutex_init(&moduleGIL, NULL);
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&moduleGIL);
+#endif
+}
+
 
 /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ## Module Keyspace Notifications API
--- a/src/module.h
+++ b/src/module.h
@@ -210,6 +210,7 @@
 void moduleAcquireGIL(void);
 int moduleTryAcquireGIL(void);
 void moduleReleaseGIL(void);
+void moduleResetGILAfterFork(void);
 void moduleNotifyKeyspaceEvent(int type, const char *event, robj *key, int dbid);
 unsigned long moduleNotifyKeyspaceSubscribersCnt(void);
 void firePostExecutionUnitJobs(void);
--- a/src/server.c
+++ b/src/server.c
@@ -6894,6 +6894,7 @@
 
     if (valkey_fork() != 0) exit(0); /* parent exits */
     setsid();                        /* create a new session */
+    moduleResetGILAfterFork();       /* the parent's GIL is not ours (QNX) */
 
